WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
If you're tired of retyping the same instructions session after session, custom slash commands are the solution.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
In this video, we'll cover what they are, how to create them, where the files live, how file naming controls the keyword,

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
and how the arguments placeholder lets you handle variable input.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
By the end, you'll be able to build commands your entire team can use.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:32.000
A slash command isn't a feature you switch on, it's a file you write, and that file's name becomes the command.

6
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.952
The slash keyword triggers an instruction template you wrote, stored as a markdown file under

7
00:00:37.952 --> 00:00:39.936
.clod/.commands. You create it.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
One thing worth knowing up front is that custom commands have since merged into skills.

9
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.920
Command files still work exactly as they always did, but the current recommended form is a skill.md

10
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.920
file under .clod/.skills.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
We'll focus on commands here, because the mechanics carry straight over.

12
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
And args is the placeholder you drop into a command file, where you want variable input to land.

13
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.000
When someone runs the command and types text after the keyword, that text replaces args in your template.

14
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
The most important conceptual point about slash commands is what they actually are.

15
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.000
They're shortcuts.

16
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
They map a keyword to a markdown file you wrote.

17
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.521
When you invoke slash deploy-review, clod code reads that file, treats its contents as a prompt, and

18
00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.934
runs it against your current session.

19
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Now, a lot of things ship with clod code out of the box.

20
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.750
Built-in commands like slash init and slash help, plus bundled skills like slash code-review and

21
00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.929
slash loop, those aren't yours.

22
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
They come with the tool.

23
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Everything else?

24
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Anything named for your own team's workflows, like slash deploy-review or slash run-tests, those are custom commands.

25
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.000
You created them.

26
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
The distinction between what ships with the tool and what you create matters, so stay sharp on it.

27
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Slash commands come in two scopes, and the scope is determined entirely by where the file lives.

28
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Project-level commands live in a .clod slash commands directory inside your project.

29
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.722
Because that directory gets committed to version control, the commands are automatically available

30
00:02:22.722 --> 00:02:24.941
to every developer who opens the repo.

31
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.000
There's no manual sharing step and no extra configuration.

32
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
User-level commands live in .clod slash commands in your home directory.

33
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Those are personal.

34
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
They're not committed anywhere, and they follow you across every project you open on your machine.

35
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.060
Both scopes use the same markdown file format and the same file name to keyword mapping, so location

36
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.939
is the only thing that changes.

37
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.000
If the workflow belongs to the team, go project-level.

38
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
If it's a personal shortcut, go user-level.

39
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
File naming is how you set the command keyword, and it's beautifully simple.

40
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Drop a file called deploy-review.md into .clod slash commands, and slash deploy-review is now a valid command.

41
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Rename it to review-pr.md, and the command becomes slash review-pr.

42
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
There's no configuration file to update, no registration step, nothing else to do.

43
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
The file name is the keyword full-stop.

44
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.000
That simplicity is also a design constraint.

45
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Choose names that are descriptive enough to be self-documenting.

46
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.000
The keyword should signal the action without requiring anyone to open the file first.

47
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:40.000
If someone runs slash do it and gets unexpected output, the name didn't do its job.

48
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Naming conventions are worth taking seriously for exactly that reason.

49
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.000
The args placeholder is what turns a static template into a flexible tool.

50
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Say you have a review-pr.md file that reviews pull requests.

51
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Without args, it would review whatever context clod already has.

52
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.000
With args, you write something like review-pull-request-args for security issues.

53
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:14.000
Now, when someone types slash review-pr 4521, the number 4521 replaces args in the template before clod sees it.

54
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
You can reference args multiple times if needed.

55
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Freeform input works well when a single piece of text is enough.

56
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.326
If your command needs structured fields with multiple values, build a more explicit template that

57
00:04:28.326 --> 00:04:30.934
guides the user toward the right format.

58
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
A quick but important note on built-in commands.

59
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.000
Clod code ships with a whole set of them, many of them bundled skills.

60
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Slash init generates a starter clod.md file for a new project.

61
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Slash help shows you the available commands.

62
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.000
And there are others like slash code review and slash loop that come with the tool, too.

63
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:58.000
The fastest way to see the full set is to type a single slash and let the list appear.

64
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.000
The practical advice is simple.

65
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Check that list before you name a custom command.

66
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.000
If you reuse a built-in name, your own file gets shadowed, and that's confusing to debug.

67
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.000
For any unused name you choose, the filename to keyword mapping is fully yours to control.

68
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.808
Custom slash commands earn their keep because they turn instructions you keep retyping into

69
00:05:22.808 --> 00:05:25.936
something a whole team can trigger with one word.

70
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Where you put the file decides who has access to it.

71
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:36.000
The filename decides what people type, and arguments decides how much the command can flex.

72
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.000
None of that takes setup beyond dropping a file into the right directory.

73
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.000
And that closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

